    </exercise>
    <exercise number="63" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>Find constants $$A$$ and $$B$$ such that the function $$y = A\sin x + B\cos x$$ satisfies the differential equation $$y^{''} + y^{\prime} - 2y = \sin x$$ .</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>$$A = - \frac{3}{10}$$ , $$B = - \frac{1}{10}$$</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="64" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <list type="ordered">
          <item>Evaluate $$\lim\limits_{x\rightarrow\infty}x\sin\frac{1}{x}$$ .</item>
          <item>Evaluate $$\lim\limits_{x\rightarrow 0}x\sin\frac{1}{x}$$ .</item>
          <item>Illustrate parts (a) and (b) by graphing $$y = x\sin\left( {1 \slash x} \right)$$ .</item>
        </list>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="65" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>Differentiate each trigonometric identity to obtain a new (or familiar) identity.</paragraph>
        <list type="ordered">
          <item>$$\tan x = \frac{\sin x}{\cos x}$$</item>
          <item>$$\sec x = \frac{1}{\cos x}$$</item>
          <item>$$\sin x + \cos x = \frac{1 + \cot x}{\csc x}$$</item>
        </list>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>$$\sec^{2}x = \frac{1}{\cos^{2}x}$$</paragraph>
        <paragraph>$$\sec x\tan x = \frac{\sin x}{\cos^{2}x}$$</paragraph>
        <paragraph>$$\cos x - \sin x = \frac{\cot x - 1}{\csc x}$$</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="66" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>A semicircle with diameter $$PQ$$ sits on an isosceles triangle $$PQR$$ to form a region shaped like a two-dimensional ice-cream cone, as shown in the figure. If $$A(\theta)$$ is the area of the semicircle and $$B(\theta)$$ is the area of the triangle, find</paragraph>
        <paragraph math="block">$$\lim\limits_{\theta\rightarrow^{}0^{+}}\frac{A(\theta)}{B(\theta)}$$</paragraph>
        <figure>
          <image alt="Details An illustration shows an inverted isosceles triangle P Q R with base P Q and bottom vertex R. Sides P R and Q R are of length 10 centimeters each, and the angle between them is labeled theta. A semicircle is mounted on the triangle such that its diameter shares with the base P Q of the triangle. The area of the semicircular region is labeled A(theta), and the area of the triangular region is labeled B(theta)."/>
        </figure>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="67" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>The figure shows a circular arc of length $$s$$ and a chord of length $$d$$ , both subtended by a central angle $$\theta$$ . Find</paragraph>
        <paragraph math="block">$$\lim\limits_{\theta\rightarrow^{}0^{+}}\frac{s}{d}$$</paragraph>
        <figure>
          <image alt="Details A circle is shown with a central angle, a chord, and a circular arc. From the center of the circle, two radii are drawn: one slopes upward to the right, and the other slopes upward to the left. The angle between these two radii is labeled theta. Both the radii intersect the circumference of the circle. A horizontal line segment representing a chord of length d connects these two intersection points on the circle. The small circular arc above the chord is labeled s."/>
        </figure>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>$$1$$</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
